[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known as one issue. The reports in it did not share one cause.
Counted from the exported reports, they fall into two problems. The desk closed #4471 on 5 October with one
outcome for each.

<Callout>
Not one issue: one fixed, one closed as won't fix. The kb page for #4471 still says known and one fix.
</Callout>

## The two problems

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

**`parser`** ([ticket]({issues.parser})). The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes
the day first, so their dates come out flipped. The desk's engineering update: **fixed**, by parsing day first for eu locales. It reached people through the app,
by email, and through booking-v2 forms.

**`hint`** ([ticket]({issues.hint})). Only booking-v3 forms, and only three tenants. The form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under
the date field. Their people type month first, as told, and the form reads it the tenant's way. The parser fix did not
change this. The desk's engineering update: **won't fix**, because the booking-v3 form is retired.

The table counts the way the desk does: a report goes where its tenant files it. A few reports read as a one-day
move. They stay under `parser`, footnoted under the table, and [the parser ticket]({issues.parser}) asks to check them
after the fix. A time zone shift is the first thing to check: one report has the same meeting on two different days
in two cities.

## Why the merge hid it

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject. These reports share subjects such as "Booking on
the wrong date" and "Date is wrong" across both problems. Subject says what the reporter saw, not what broke.
[Triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) also shows that tenant and channel decide the cause, and intake never looks them up.
Intake's own tag disagrees with the cause for a share of the reports, so it cannot be used to split them.

## What to do

- Update the page for [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471): it is closed, with `parser` fixed and `hint` won't fix.
- Check the reports read as a one-day move against the fix. A fix for day-first parsing does not change a time zone shift.
- Write a new decision record that replaces 0001 for incidents. Do not edit 0001: it is accepted.

